<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles the Good</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1084–<a href="/years/1127">1127</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Charles I, Count of Flanders</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles the Good was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1119 until 1127. A son of King Canute IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Adela of Flanders, he was raised at the Flemish court and succeeded his cousin Baldwin VII. Known for his piety, for keeping the peace and for relieving famine, he was murdered in the church of St Donatian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> on 2 March 1127 by members of the Erembald clan, setting off a succession crisis recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galbert-of-bruges">Galbert of Bruges</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-good.jpg" alt="Charles the Good" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sint-Salvatorskathedraal_-_Bruges_-_IMG_4774.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the only son among the three children of King Canute IV and Adela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. His father was assassinated in Odense Cathedral in 1086, and Adela fled to Flanders with the very young Charles, leaving her twin daughters Ingeborg and Cecilia in Denmark. Charles grew up at the court of his grandfather Robert I of Flanders and his uncle Robert II of Flanders.</p><p>He travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in 1107 or 1108 with a fleet of English, Danish and Flemish crusaders. In 1124 a faction of nobles opposed to King Baldwin II offered him the crown of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galbert-of-bruges">Galbert of Bruges</a> he refused at the urging of advisers who feared that his departure would leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> at the mercy of the Erembald clan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Robert II died in 1111, Charles's cousin Baldwin VII became count, and Charles was a close adviser. Wounded at the Battle of Bures-en-Brai in September 1118, the childless Baldwin designated Charles as successor before dying on 17 July 1119.</p><p>In 1125 Charles was also considered a candidate for King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> after the death of Henry V but rejected the offer. During the famine of that year he had legumes planted on his own estates and given away, distributed bread to the poor, and, according to Galbert, said it was better for the rich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> to drink only water than for a single poor person to die of hunger. He cracked down on the hoarding of grain to raise prices, and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Flanders, attributing such practices to Jewish merchants. At the urging of his advisers he also began legal proceedings to reduce the wealthy Erembald family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bertulf, head of the Erembald family, a priest and provost of the Church of St. Donatian, organised a conspiracy to kill Charles and replace him with William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>. On the morning of 2 March 1127 Charles knelt in prayer in St. Donatian's, his hand full of coins to give alms. During Mass knights answering to the Erembalds entered and hacked him to death with broadswords. The murder provoked widespread outrage, and he was almost at once regarded as a martyr and saint, though not formally beatified until 1882 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>.</p><p>The Erembalds were besieged in the comital castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> by nobles and commoners of Bruges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, and all the conspirators were captured and tortured to death. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vi-of-france">Louis VI of France</a>, who had supported the uprising, then used his influence to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-clito">William Clito</a> of the House of Normandy chosen as the next count.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles the Good” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The murder of Count <b>Charles the Good</b> in the church of St Donatian at Bruges in 1127, described in detail by the notary Galbert of Bruges, set off a succession crisis in which the towns for the first time claimed a say in choosing the count.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>William Clito, imposed as Count of Flanders by Louis VI of France after the murder of <b>Charles the Good</b>, defeated his rival Thierry of Alsace at Axspoele.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-21">21 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Odense, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-donatians-cathedral">St. Donatian's Cathedral</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.4,10.38&amp;year=1111" aria-label="Odense on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles the Good"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5501 5354h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5501 5354h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.4,10.38&amp;year=1111">What was here in 1111? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
